-- Plan 84 leaf 1a: a `=>` on a signature line that is not a bound clause.
--
-- `constraints` reads the clause **speculatively** and rewinds if no `=>`
-- arrives, which is what lets `class Shape t` and `class Debug t => Shape t`
-- begin identically. The rewind was silent, so this program used to transpile
-- with the whole clause swallowed into the first parameter's verbatim type and
-- `TokenKind::FatArrow`'s own Debug name reaching the generated Rust:
--
--   fn shout<T>(x: Into<String> T FatArrow T) -> String
--
-- No diagnostic, and the `.xrs` is a human-facing artifact (doc 02 §10.1), so
-- that is a defect rather than a gap. Plan 84 leaf 0 measured four shapes
-- arriving this way and `docs/GRAMMAR.md` grades all four.
--
-- **This is the most common shape of bound in Rust** — `T: Into<String>`,
-- `U: From<T>`, `I: IntoIterator<Item = T>`, `F: Fn(i32) -> i32` — so the help
-- names the restriction rather than the token. An author who wrote this made a
-- correct guess at real Rust; "expected a type" would not tell them why it was
-- refused.

shout : Into<String> t => t -> String
shout x = .into x

main : ()
main =
  println! \-> "#{(shout "hi")}"
  ()
